====================================================================================
VERDICT: shots to reach the same RELATIVE gradient precision
====================================================================================
  cost / |g|^2 is the shot count for equal relative accuracy;
  lower is cheaper. circuits column is the other axis.

  method                  circuits        cost      |g|   cost/|g|^2  vs par-shift
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  parameter-shift               32    0.001883   3.3040     0.000173          1.00
  Hadamard k=1 n=1              16    0.476433   2.8767     0.057572        333.69
  Hadamard k=1 n=2               8    0.227949   2.8947     0.027204        157.67
  Hadamard k=1 n=4               4    0.135759   2.4791     0.022090        128.03
  Hadamard k=1 n=8               2    0.052270   2.3865     0.009178         53.19
  Hadamard k=1 n=16              1    0.028866   1.5695     0.011718         67.92
  Hadamard k=1 n=M pred          1    0.028175   1.5695     0.011438         66.29
  QPE k=4 n=1                   16    0.061598   2.9778     0.006947         40.26
  QPE k=4 n=2                    8    0.036492   2.9673     0.004144         24.02
  QPE k=4 n=4                    4    0.015566   2.5806     0.002337         13.55
  QPE k=4 n=8                    2    0.008065   2.3442     0.001468          8.51
  QPE k=4 n=M pred               1    0.003888   2.3442     0.000707          4.10

  NOTE the |g| columns: QLTO targets the SMEARED gradient, whose norm is
  0.79-0.93 of the exact one at R=0.6, so a like-for-like relative
  comparison must divide each method's cost by its OWN |g|^2. The raw
  cost column is only comparable within a method.
